    "turn_idx": 50,
    "turn_text": "And the, on the video, Chairman John Whitmire, a Democrat, introduced him. He's the chairman of Criminal Jurisprudence in the Senate. He had watched the video. And he came up, I talked to him when I was logging about it. He said, oh, listen, Pat. I saw their video, and I saw their testimony. I'm supportive of your bill. You will get to hear it. And I have every intention of passing it out of my committee. So the long story there, I mean, long story short on the Senate side, was we passed that bill out of the Senate unanimously, 31 to 0. Very proud of it. Now, unfortunately, James, the House is just different, you know, and it's a different dynamic here, different players. His bill died in the House. And it was late in the session. Fortunately, we had the life raft, which was our bill coming out of the Senate. So he picked that up, and it was still having problems. So we had to, James and myself, went through the chair of the committee in the House. He did Democrat. And we said, listen, we won't, our version of the Senate had an expansion of the death penalty, because essentially we've said, yes, you murder a child under the age of 15 instead of 10, which would have covered Lord. that you will face either the death penalty or life without the possibility for all. And she wouldn't hear the bill if it was being expanded. So after talking to Linda Vajos and really thinking about it, praying on it, I said, well, what if we made it like without the possibility of parole? Because at least we know that this person is never going to harm any child again. And she said, if you do that, then I'll hear the bill. So she heard it, it passed out a committee, and then, believe it or not, it got stuck in the calendar committee, which is the second committee, the way the house was, where they set the schedule. So any house bill in Texas really have to have two yes vote, one, whatever committee it's assigned to, and then another, to get it on the calendar itself, and hence the name of the committee and the challenge committee. So they were, it was stalled there. No, I promise that I called Vernon Bianca and I said, listen, today I'm going to go and talk to every single one of those members individually that's 15 of them, because I was a House member and I know them all personally. And I went and spoke to them and explained that this was the most important bill for our office. And I played the American bill. And finally, it got set on the calendar. And it didn't get passed out unanimously, but it's overwhelming. I believe 132 to 8. And we finally got a pass. And then James and I went to the conference, ironed out the differences, and the Governor shines the bill with the Land of Oz's presence. This passed you.",
